<%# The qualifying note on ONE statement about a package: jurisdiction,
    training, retention. Third appearance of the same idiom; the trigger had
    already been noted in confirm_package.html.erb ("unify at the third").

    The locale file is the data. A value without an entry renders nothing,
    instead of a branch per value having to be maintained here. A newly added
    value therefore falls into "no note needed", which would be exactly the
    silent all-clear the card is built against. That is why a guard spec pins
    that every value except the harmless one has an entry.

    Deliberately the same font size as the description (§7): small print would
    undermine the honesty these notes exist for.

    Locals:
      scope    : locale prefix of the note group
      value    : the value whose note is looked up (empty = nothing claimed)
      args     : optional interpolation (retention names a number of days)
      fallback : text for the case that `value` is empty. Only the switch dialog
                 needs it: there "nothing classified" is a statement of its own,
                 on the card it stays silent. %>
<% args = local_assigns.fetch(:args, {}) %>
<% note = if value.present?
            t("#{scope}.#{value}", default: nil, **args)
          else
            local_assigns[:fallback]
          end %>
<%# A pluralised entry that is looked up WITHOUT `count` returns the raw
    hash, and `present?` is true for it. Without this line,
    `{:one=>"…", :other=>"…"}` would show as text on the customer card. Retention
    can no longer run into this (derived_retention downgrades „befristet" without
    a number to „nicht zugesichert"), but the next group someone pluralises
    should not get stuck on it again. %>
<% note = nil unless note.is_a?(String) %>
<% if note.present? %>
  <p class="mt-2 text-sm text-cream-800"><%= note %></p>
<% end %>
